<IndustryResource title="Carrier Offload for Gyms and Fitness Portfolios" description="How gyms can evaluate Wi-Fi offload with long dwell time, repeat members, connected fitness equipment, peak capacity, and portfolio reporting." h1="Can Gyms Monetize Member Wi-Fi Through Carrier Offload?" answer="Gyms can be attractive carrier-offload candidates because members return often and may stay for extended periods. Qualification still depends on eligible subscriber devices, program availability, compatible Wi-Fi, backhaul, and secure separation from access control, payments, cameras, staff, and connected fitness equipment." path="/carrier-offload-for-gyms/" source="carrier-offload-for-gyms" audience="Fitness clubs, health-club chains, boutique studios, recreation centers, and multi-location operators combine repeat visitors with predictable morning and evening demand." opportunity="Longer workouts can support data use, but member behavior differs across strength, cardio, studio, pool, and recovery areas. Existing published gym figures should not be generalized without evidence and site-specific inputs." operations={[{title:'Repeat membership',text:'Recurring visits can create consistent patterns, but the same device must still be eligible and automatically authorized.'},{title:'Connected equipment',text:'Fitness machines, casting, audio, cameras, access control, and member apps require clear network ownership and segmentation.'},{title:'Peak concurrency',text:'Before-work and after-work peaks need RF and backhaul testing, not just daily-member totals.'},{title:'Portfolio variation',text:'Boutique studios, big-box gyms, urban clubs, and recreation facilities have different dwell time, layouts, and networks.'}]} qualification={['Check-ins, unique daily members, peak concurrency, visit frequency, and median dwell time','Coverage and capacity across workout floors, studios, locker areas, reception, and parking where relevant','AP/controller/firmware, internet capacity, QoS, 